# webapp-testing (Cursor)

Turns Playwright patterns into files you actually run, wired through one
Python entrypoint. For granular Playwright patterns see `e2e-testing`; for
the scenario list to test see `test-case-matrix` (run first, each item
becomes one RED step); for the DB/data hygiene mechanics see `api-testing`.

## When NOT to use

Just need Playwright patterns/snippets by hand — use `e2e-testing`. Pure
unit/integration TDD with no browser — use `test-driven-development`.

## Steps

1. **Install scaffolding**: copy the Playwright config to project root,
   the runner + report-builder scripts into `scripts/`, add
   `@playwright/test` as a dev dependency. In the same pass, add
   `docs/qa/playwright-report/`, `docs/qa/test-results/`,
   `docs/qa/playwright-results.json`, and `docs/qa/*/test-matrix.html` to
   `.gitignore` — mandatory, not optional, or the first run stages
   megabytes of screenshots/video into git. Only
   `docs/qa/<slug>/test-matrix.md` should stay tracked under `docs/qa/`.

2. **Write the test first (RED)**. Before implementing a new flow, write a
   spec under `tests/e2e/` describing the desired behavior — it should
   fail. Before writing a flow that creates or depends on data, check who
   owns that entity: if this repo owns it, proceed; if another
   repo/service owns it, stop and ask for its API contract or a staging
   endpoint rather than guessing its shape. Tag whatever data the test
   creates (a `run_id` or fixed prefix) for later cleanup.

   Use a `stepShot` wrapper around each logical action (not a flat
   `await`-chain) so the report shows a per-step breakdown matching the
   test case's `Test Steps` in `test-case-matrix`. If the source matrix is
   in Bahasa Indonesia, keep the test title and every `stepShot` label in
   Bahasa Indonesia too — the report is read side-by-side with the matrix,
   a language mismatch defeats that. Keep id prefixes and selector
   literals untranslated.

3. **Run via the runner**, not raw `npx playwright test` — it wraps the
   configured reporters, prints a pass/fail/flaky summary, then
   post-processes the report to add video speed controls. Runs headed by
   default (visible browser) so you can watch it work; pass `--headless`
   for unattended/agent-driven runs. Up to 3 parallel workers even when
   headed; CI stays pinned to 1.

4. **Implement until green**, re-running after each change, then refactor
   with tests still green.

5. **Optional local pre-push gate**: install the provided git hook to gate
   `git push` on the suite passing — purely local, no CI/workflow files,
   opt-in only (don't install unless asked).

## Report

The HTML report is Playwright's own unmodified `html` reporter output,
post-processed to add slow-motion video controls (default 0.5×, with
speed buttons) and a floating "Test Matrix" menu linking every
`docs/qa/<slug>/test-matrix.md` rendered to a sibling `.html`. Open via
`npx playwright show-report docs/qa/playwright-report` (needs serving, not
double-clicking `index.html`, since attachments live in a sibling `data/`
folder).

## Test data hygiene (application data, not report artifacts)

A browser-driven test is always the "real DB, black-box" case —
transaction rollback never applies. Tag every record created (a `run_id`
or fixed prefix), add a `globalTeardown` deleting everything tagged with
that run's id through the app's own API, and keep a standalone idempotent
cleanup script for crashed runs. Never report a suite "set up" or
"passing" while it creates real records with neither of these in place.
